        {
          "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/bitcoin/bitcoin/git/commits/02d2134d5f78d71b1fe824947fee368952c361ed",
          "sha": "02d2134d5f78d71b1fe824947fee368952c361ed",
          "html_url": "https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/commit/02d2134d5f78d71b1fe824947fee368952c361ed"
        }
      ],
      "message": "memusage: model real malloc implementations in MallocUsage\n\nThe previous formula was measured on glibc 2.19 and overestimates the\nsize of glibc chunks by 16 bytes for half of all request sizes, ignores\nglibc's minimum chunk size, and has no notion of mmapped allocations.\n\nModel the allocators actually used by Linux, macOS and Windows builds:\n- glibc (ptmalloc2, all Linux release builds): chunks of\n  max(MINSIZE, round_up(size + sizeof(size_t), MALLOC_ALIGNMENT)) bytes,\n  with chunks of 128 KiB or more mmapped as whole pages. This matches\n  glibc 2.43 exactly on x86_64, i386, aarch64, armhf, armel, riscv64,\n  ppc64, ppc64le, ppc, s390x, loongarch64, alpha, hppa, m68k, sh4 and\n  sparc64 (over 300000 request sizes each).\n- musl (mallocng, used by the Alpine CI job): a table of the steady-state\n  cost per slot for each of its 48 size classes, including group headers\n  and out-of-band metadata, which matches RSS measurements of musl 1.2.5\n  within 0.2%.\n- macOS (libmalloc): no in-band headers, with requests rounded up to 16\n  bytes up to 1008 bytes (tiny/nano), to 512 bytes up to 32 KiB (small),\n  to 32 KiB up to 8 MiB (medium), and to whole pages beyond. This matches\n  the CI measurements on macOS 15 (arm64) for every size the previous\n  estimate got wrong (1-112, 1025-7200 and 32769-493424); the other sizes\n  up to 4 MiB were within the test's tolerance. The 8 MiB limit of the\n  medium range is libmalloc's default (from its source), not measured.\n- Windows x64 (the Windows heap, used by the UCRT's malloc): blocks of\n  round_up(size + 8, 16) bytes, except that sizes up to 16 KiB come from\n  its low-fragmentation heap, in buckets of 16 to 512 bytes granularity.\n  This matches the CI measurements on Windows Server 2022 up to 512 KiB\n  (beyond that, addresses no longer reflect usage). Blocks of over\n  0xFF000 bytes get their own VirtualAlloc() region, and are counted as\n  their committed pages (not measured).\n\nOther platforms (BSDs) keep the previous estimate until they are\nmeasured.\n\nAlso count the hash that libc++ stores in every std::unordered_map and\nstd::unordered_set node, and the second list pointer of MSVC's STL. The\nprevious MallocUsage() overestimated small allocations enough to hide\nthis, but with the accurate models, pool_tests/memusage_test fails with\nlibc++ (it finds that the pool allocator uses more memory than\nstd::allocator, as the std_map nodes are counted as 24 instead of 32\nbytes), and would with MSVC.\n\nThat test also assumes that malloc has a per-allocation overhead, which\nthe pool allocator avoids. macOS's allocator has none for small blocks,\nso there the pool really uses slightly more memory (for the list of its\nchunks). Only check that the pool saves memory where malloc's overhead\nfor the nodes is significant.",
