      "message": "test: compare MallocUsage against measured allocator behaviour\n\nAdd a unit test that measures how much memory malloc() actually consumes\nand fails if MallocUsage() is more than a factor 1+1/16 below, or more\nthan a factor 1+1/8 above, the measured usage. The upper bound is looser\nbecause MallocUsage() may include out-of-band allocator metadata that\nthe measurement does not see (up to 1/8 for musl's smallest size class).\n\nBy default, a random set of sizes up to 4 MiB (512 KiB on Windows, see\nbelow) is tested: for every power of two 2^b, 16 >> (b / 4) (but at\nleast one) sizes in [2^b, 2^(b+1)), so small sizes are tested more\ndensely than large ones. Every mismatching size is reported on its own\nline, with its measured usage.\n\nWhen the ALL_SIZES constant is set to true, the test instead determines\nthe usage of every size up to 4 MiB (searching for the boundaries\nbetween size classes, assuming usage does not decrease with size),\nreports mismatching size ranges, and prints the results in a form meant\nfor writing MallocUsage() code, e.g. for glibc on x86_64:\n\n  1-24: 32\n  25-131048: round_up(size + 8, 16)\n  131049-4194280: round_up(size + 24, 4096)\n\nThis takes about 25 seconds with glibc, and 5 with musl. In this mode,\nglibc's mmap threshold is pinned to its default, as glibc otherwise\nraises it whenever an mmapped block is freed, which would make the\nresults depend on the order of the measurements.\n\nThe usage of a size is measured as the smallest distance between\nneighbouring blocks (in address order), among up to 16384 live\nallocations of that size, that accounts for at least 10% of all pairs.\nThis is robust against a fragmented heap (the test binary's heap holds\nover a thousand regularly spaced free holes at startup, which dominate\nsmaller samples) and against allocators that place blocks at varying\noffsets within their slots (musl).\n\nLarge blocks are often given page-granular memory, with the block at an\noffset within it that varies from block to block (musl cycles it), which\nmakes neighbouring blocks slightly closer than the memory they use. Such\nan allocator returns a different address, less than a page away, when a\nblock is freed and allocated again. If that happens (for distances of a\npage or more), the test also looks at the longest run of neighbours\nwhose distances are within a page of that distance. If all of them lie\nwithin less than a page of a grid whose spacing is a multiple of the\npage size, that spacing is the usage. Blocks that are really spaced by\nsomething else (e.g. glibc's 4080-byte chunks) drift away from any such\ngrid, though not necessarily within a short run (such as within one\nWindows heap segment), which is why this is only done when the offsets\nare seen to vary. Without this, musl's output in the ALL_SIZES mode has\ntwo lines per page above its mmap threshold, one of them 16 bytes too\nlow; with it, the output is:\n\n  1-12: 16\n  13-156: round_up(size + 4, 16)\n  157-188: 192\n  ... (one line per size class) ...\n  104829-131036: 131072\n  131037-131051: 131056\n  131052-131052: 131072\n  131053-4194284: round_up(size + 20, 4096)\n\nOn Windows, only sizes up to 512 KiB are tested: larger blocks often get\ntheir own heap segment or VirtualAlloc() region, so the distances\nbetween them reflect reserved address space rather than usage.\n\nSanitizer builds are skipped, as MallocUsage() does not model their\nallocators.",
